# SOLID Principles and Function Design

## Purpose
One standard for designing and reviewing classes and functions. The thresholds below are the ones the code-quality reviewer applies. A writer who stays inside them gets no finding, and a reviewer reports nothing inside them.

## When To Apply
Apply when creating or extending classes, services, modules or functions, and when reviewing them.

---

## Thresholds (shared with review-clean-code)

| Signal | Threshold | When a reviewer reports it |
|---|---|---|
| Function body length | over 40 lines | Only when the function also mixes levels of abstraction or does two separable jobs. A long linear table, serializer or test is not a finding. |
| Positional parameters | 4 or more | When there is no options object and the parameters are not a natural ordered set the codebase already uses. |
| Boolean flag parameter | any | When the flag selects between two different behaviours, so that call sites read `run(x, true)`. |
| Constructor dependencies | more than 5 | As an SRP signal; name the two responsibilities. |
| Branch chain over a type discriminant | 4 or more branches, extended by the change | When the codebase already uses a lookup map or a strategy for the same kind of dispatch. |
| Duplicated block | 3 or more copies | Reported once, listing every copy. |

Under a threshold, the reviewer stays silent. At or over one, the finding is `minor` unless it names a trigger and a wrong outcome (see the review-orchestrator severity rubric).

---

## S — Single Responsibility
**One class or module, one reason to change.**
- Do not mix fetching, business rules and presentation or persistence in one class or function.
- If you cannot name it in three words without "and", it does too much.
- A constructor assigns. It does not perform I/O or start async work.

## O — Open/Closed
- Adding a variant should not mean editing a growing `if/else` or `switch` when the codebase already dispatches that kind of thing through a map or a strategy.

## L — Liskov Substitution
- An override must work wherever the base type is used. An override that throws "not supported", or rejects input the base accepts, breaks callers that hold the base type.

## I — Interface Segregation
- An implementation that leaves methods as stubs or no-ops means the interface is too wide.

## D — Dependency Inversion
- Business logic receives the dependencies it calls into (I/O, network, clock) instead of constructing them. Inside a module, constructing a plain helper or value object is fine.

---

## Iron Laws

**IRON LAW 1: An override never throws where its base type would not.**
**IRON LAW 2: A constructor performs no I/O.**
**IRON LAW 3: The thresholds above are the standard for both writing and review. Change them here, never in a reviewer.**
